":"with"}],"gloss":"with longing"},{"surface":"இரவலர்","parts":[{"text":"இரவலர்","lemma":"iravalar","gloss":"suppliants"}],"gloss":"suppliants"},{"surface":"வருவர்","parts":[{"text":"வரு","lemma":"varu","gloss":"to come"},{"text":"வ்","lemma":"v","gloss":"future tense marker"},{"text":"அர்","lemma":"ar","gloss":"third person plural suffix"}],"gloss":"they will come"},{"surface":"அது","parts":[{"text":"அது","lemma":"atu","gloss":"that"}],"gloss":"that"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"முன்னம்","parts":[{"text":"முன்னம்","lemma":"muṉṉam","gloss":"intention"}],"gloss":"intention"},{"surface":"முகத்தின்","parts":[{"text":"முகத்தின்","lemma":"முகத்தின்","gloss":"through the face"}],"gloss":"through the face","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"உணர்ந்து","parts":[{"text":"உணர்ந்து","lemma":"உணர்ந்து","gloss":"understanding"}],"gloss":"understanding","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"அவர்","parts":[{"text":"அவர்","lemma":"avar","gloss":"their"}],"gloss":"their"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"இன்மை","parts":[{"text":"இன்மை","lemma":"iṉmai","gloss":"poverty"}],"gloss":"poverty"},{"surface":"தீர்த்தல்","parts":[{"text":"தீர்","lemma":"tīr","gloss":"to end"},{"text":"த்","lemma":"t","gloss":"transitive marker"},{"text":"த்","lemma":"t","gloss":"transitive marker"},{"text":"அல்","lemma":"al","gloss":"verbal noun suffix"}],"gloss":"removing"},{"surface":"வன்மையானே","parts":[{"text":"வன்மை","lemma":"vaṉmai","gloss":"generosity"},{"text":"ஆன்","lemma":"āṉ","gloss":"by"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ē","gloss":"indeed"}],"gloss":"indeed by generosity"}]}],"literal":"Lofty white umbrella like the form of the full moon / shading the earth bounded by the moonlit ocean, / while the protective drum roars with the sound 'izhum', / O descendant of the Pandyas of unceasing generosity, who directed the wheel of authority with a loving heart! / O husband of the beautifully jeweled one of blemishless chastity! / On the dark, strong nape of the neck of the great-trunked elephant, / which has a beautiful forehead adorned with spots and a golden plate, / and fragrant rut of strength difficult to approach, / using its tusks as weapons to break the fort doors, / having hanging bells on its sides bound with ropes, / being seated on top, / O Vazhuti of great fame, with a strong hand and a bright sword, unwearied in the difficult task like the remediless God of Death! / Even if the earth moves, your word does not change, / O broad-chested one, vast and wide, with dried sandalwood paste on your chest and golden anklets on your legs! / Suppliants will come with longing and desire for you, / crossing the difficult, distressful paths without villages, / long paths without water, / where fierce men of perfect, unfailing aim, watching from their abodes with downward-looking eyes, / discharge arrows at travelers, who fall and are buried under new stone cairns, / where vultures with well-formed wings and curved beaks sit and grieve / on the Unnam trees at the difficult-to-approach forked paths. / Understanding their intentions through their faces, / removing their poverty is indeed your generosity.","translation":"Your lofty white umbrella, like the full moon, shades the earth bounded by the moonlit ocean, while your protective drum roars with a deep resonance. O descendant of the Pandyas, who rules with a loving heart and unceasing generosity! O husband of the beautifully jeweled queen of blemishless chastity! Seated on the dark, strong neck of your great elephant—whose forehead is adorned with a golden plate and spots, whose fragrant rut makes it difficult to approach, and whose tusks break open fort gates while bells chime on its rope-bound sides—you are the famous Vazhuti, wielding a bright sword in your strong hand, as unyielding in battle as the God of Death. Even if the earth moves, your word never changes, O broad-chested king adorned with sandalwood paste and golden anklets! Suppliants will come to you, driven by their longing for your grace. They cross long, waterless, and desolate paths where fierce highwaymen with unfailing aim shoot down travelers, leaving them under stone cairns where vultures sit on Unnam trees and grieve. When they arrive, reading their needs from their faces and removing their poverty is indeed the true mark of your greatness.","modern":"முழுநிலவு போன்ற உன்னுடைய உயர்ந்த வெண்கொற்றக்குடை, நிலவு வீசும் கடலால் சூழப்பட்ட இந்த உலகத்திற்கு நிழல் தருகிறது. உன்னுடைய காவல் முரசம் அதிர்ந்து முழங்குகிறது. அன்பான நெஞ்சோடு செங்கோல் செலுத்தும், கொடையில் சிறந்த பாண்டியர் மரபில் வந்தவனே! குற்றமற்ற கற்புடைய அரசியின் கணவனே! பொன்னால் ஆன நெற்றிப்பட்டமும் புள்ளிகளும் உடைய, மதம் பொழியும் பெரிய யானையின் கழுத்தில் அமர்ந்து, எமனின் தொழிலைப் போல பகைவரின் கோட்டைக் கதவுகளைத் தகர்க்கும் வலிமை மிக்க வாளேந்திய வழுதியே! பூமி பெயர்ந்தாலும் உனது சொல் தவறாத மார்பினை உடையவனே! ஊர்களும் நீரும் இல்லாத நீண்ட பாலைவன வழியில், வழிப்பறி செய்யும் வில்லினரின் அம்புகளுக்குப் பலியாகி விழுந்தவர்களின் நடுகற்கள் மீது பருந்துகள் அமர்ந்து கதறும் வழிகளைக் கடந்து, உன்னை நாடி இரவலர்கள் வருவார்கள். அவர்களின் குறிப்பை முகத்திலேயே அறிந்து, அவர்களின் வறுமையைப் போக்குவதே உனது சிறந்த கொடைத்தன்மையாகும்.","explanation":"The poet Irumpitarttalaiyar addresses the Pandya king Karuṅkai Oḷvāḷ Perumpeyar Vazhuti in this poem of praise and advice. He begins by extolling the king's righteous rule, symbolized by his white umbrella that shades the earth, and his noble lineage. The poet describes the king's fearsome valor in battle, riding a war elephant to break down enemy fort gates, and praises his unyielding commitment to his word. He then paints a vivid picture of the perilous desert path (pālai) that suppliants must cross to reach the king, filled with fierce highwaymen, graves, and grieving vultures. Finally, the poet advises the king that his true greatness lies in anticipating the needs of these poor suppliants by reading their faces and completely removing their poverty. Key words include வழுதி (Vazhuti, the king), இரவலர் (suppliants), இன்மை (poverty), and வன்மை (generosity).","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}